\\\\ C & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d61be35e-6d1f-53f2-b082-5c2589abd825" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504724}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504739 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ea8c6ee-eebb-554f-96d7-fedb390662a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504085} {:answer/id #uuid "2816ab7c-d852-592e-8650-840079486585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504102} {:answer/id #uuid "9544b926-69e9-5ceb-9bae-c0f2023254f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504298} {:answer/id #uuid "af92b5f0-d155-51a2-b88f-351e8cef4aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504335} {:answer/id #uuid "d8be9b06-c8cf-557d-aeed-4ec45c6e90d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504412}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504335} :answer-field/id #uuid "caf9993d-ed7b-5673-ab78-2cccfa1d0f6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d4f26408-d253-562e-8b0f-c05af2f72107" :question/math-academy-id "q-32688" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $C$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\n$$\n\nGiven that $C$ has the LU factorization $C=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ C & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dec2dbf2-e550-5e9f-9032-4f396f415ee3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504733}}] :activity/title "LU Factorization of 3x3 Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504025}]}
